                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This current Steam version of the game is not the original version, it’s a cut version. The developers cut content, riddles out of the game, but they were not thorough enough. When they cut a riddle from the game, they did not always tie up all loose ends. This way it could happen that one character gave me instructions on how to solve the next riddle (the developers forgot to delete this info from the character’s dialogue) and when I got to that point, I started trying to solve the riddle by following the instructions received earlier. But there was no riddle to be solved, because they already cut it, and nobody told that to me, and I spent long hours trying to figure out that despite following the earlier verbal instructions why nothing happened, why I could not solve the riddle, what I was doing wrong. \n\nAlso, sometimes they are not consistent with how they refer to an item in the game. One of the characters calls it something, but it appears in my inventory under a quite different name, and I did not know that that character was referring to this item in her earlier dialogue, and this is why it did not dawn on me that I had to use this item to gain access to a certain place. \n\nThe small footprints icon that indicates that Ariane can go to a certain direction does not always show up when it has to show up. The footprints icon is helpful, because it shows that you can go to a new place, there is a new place that should be explored, but when it does not show up, the player might think that there is not anything in that direction to explore, so he/she does not go to that direction and this way he/she misses important places/info/items that are needed to progress with the game. So, the player should always check those directions as well that seemingly lead to nowhere because the missing footprints did not show up.\n\nYou have to be careful as to where to go with Ariane. If she does not follow the right path, she can get stuck, even if she went to a perfectly accessible place. If this happens you have to load an earlier save, and if you did not save frequently, you can lose a lot of progress and you have to repeat it all again.\n\nAn old, story-driven point-and-click adventure game inspired by Jules Verne’s novel, the graphics are obviously dated, but if you like this type of games, then it's an enjoyable adventure, it's worth playing. Recommended. \n",
